Chapter: BoundedNoneExceeded
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model, the bottom rung. `bounded none` promises the call allocates
 nothing, and this chapter is its refusal arm.

 The refusal must be TRANSITIVE, which is the half a self-contained arm would
 miss. `counts` allocates nothing you can see -- its whole body is one call --
 and it is refused because `sum-pushed` builds a list literal and `build-list`
 pushes onto it. That is the same shape the `linear` arm is built around, one
 rung down: clean where you read it, allocating underneath.

Section: The Callee That Allocates

  build-list : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  build-list (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else build-list n (i + 1) (list-push acc i)

  sum-pushed : Integer -> Integer
  sum-pushed (n) = list-length (build-list n 0 [])

Section: The Declaration That Cannot Hold

  bounded none counts : Integer -> Integer
  counts (n) = sum-pushed n

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (show (counts 4))
  end
